    "abstract": "Current advanced research on infrared and visible image fusion (IVIF) primarily focuses on improving fusion performance under normal illumination, while its applicability in low-light conditions remains underexplored. To address this, we propose ULightIF, an unsupervised IVIF framework tailored for low-light scenarios. Our method innovatively leverages the pre-enhanced results from the “enhance first, fuse later” process to guide the “fuse first, enhance later” procedure, thereby overcoming the limitations of existing low-light fusion methods that typically rely on a single strategy. From a structural perspective, ULightIF integrates low-light enhancement, multimodal image fusion, and image decomposition subnetworks within a flexible architecture. It further adopts a convolutional re-parameterization technique, resulting in a lightweight network with only approximately 0.0597 M parameters. The network is trained using a carefully designed set of unsupervised loss functions, using only paired low-light infrared and visible images without requiring normally illuminated data. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves a favorable balance between high-quality visual performance and adaptability to downstream tasks, while maintaining high efficiency for practical deployment.",
